  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Space Exploration Technologies has $2.8B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, across 80 awards with an average award size of $35.6M, and obligations are down 5.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $2.2B in obligations, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $684M. Obligations grew from $1.5B in FY21 to $3.4B in FY24, then moved to $3B in FY25 and are $2.8B in FY26. The company’s work is concentrated in Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $1.6B, Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing at $519M, and Satellite Telecommunications at $474M. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences accounts for $250M, and other listed NAICS sectors register much smaller totals. The current-year totals are fiscal year to date and therefore partial. This snapshot shows which agencies and NAICS markets account for the bulk of federal obligations to Space Exploration Technologies so far in FY2026.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top buying agencies for Space Exploration Technologies?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $2.2B to Space Exploration Technologies, making it the dominant buyer. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $684M. The Department of State obligated $3.2M and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $1.5M. The Department of Justice obligated $600K, and the Department of Transportation obligated $9K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which markets and NAICS sectors receive the most obligations?",
        "body": "Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation is the largest market with $1.6B in obligations. Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing and Satellite Telecommunications follow with $519M and $474M respectively. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences accounts for $250M, with remaining listed sectors at much smaller dollar levels."
      }
    ]
  },
